# Simfinity.js Architecture

Simfinity generates GraphQL schemas, CRUD queries and mutations from `GraphQLObjectType` definitions. MongoDB and PostgreSQL adapters share the GraphQL runtime and provide their own storage implementations. Backend selection is fixed for each runtime instance.

## Tech Stack

- **Language**: JavaScript (ES modules, `"type": "module"`)
- **Runtime**: Node.js >= 18.18.0 for consumers; Node.js 24 for development/CI
- **GraphQL**: `graphql` ^16 (peer dependency)
- **Databases**: MongoDB via `mongoose` ^8; PostgreSQL >=15 via `pg` ^8
- **Testing**: Vitest
- **Linting**: ESLint 9 (flat config)
- **Server integration**: GraphQL Yoga with Envelop plugins

## Workspace boundaries

The root `simfinity-workspace` package is private and contains development commands only. Every publishable library has its own `package.json`, `src/`, `types/`, `README.md` and `LICENSE` under `packages/`. Shared tests remain at the root. The independent VitePress project in `docs/` reads the root version without installing a runtime package.

`examples/barber/mongodb`, `examples/barber/postgres`, and `examples/barber/frontend` are private applications with independent npm manifests and lockfiles outside the library workspaces. The backends consume released Simfinity runtime packages at exactly 3.5.9; no file dependencies, vendor archives, or npm publication are involved. The two Compose projects select separate backends at startup and share frontend source, while native Mongoose and PostgreSQL code remains in its owning backend. See `simfinity-barber-examples.mdc` for their operational and test boundaries.

All five package versions, exact internal dependencies and the private root version stay aligned through `scripts/release-packages.js`. Publication order is core, SQL, MCP, PostgreSQL, MongoDB. Pushing a `v*` release tag or publishing a GitHub release runs `release.yml`, which validates that the existing tag matches aligned versions and source merged into `master`, validates packages and real databases, calls reusable `publish.yml`, verifies npm integrity/visibility, creates or updates the GitHub release and deploys stable documentation. Version changes and merges do not publish; manual dispatch is preview-only. Restrict the `npm-release` environment to `v*` tags and allow Pages from release tags plus `master` for documentation-only deployments. npm uses OIDC trusted publishing for caller `release.yml` and environment `npm-release`; keep local credentials and `NPMJS_TOKEN` out of this flow. Preserve superseded-run guards before registry/latest/Pages mutations and retry-safe artifacts. Keep local archives outside the repository; `docs/public/` contains intentional downloadable artifacts.

- `packages/core` (`@simtlix/simfinity-core`) owns driver-free `createRuntime`, model metadata, query plans, scalar factory, errors, filter types and the once-only introspection patch. Registries/middleware are per runtime instance. Its public subpaths `./auth`, `./auth/errors`, `./auth/expressions`, `./auth/rules`, `./plugins`, `./scalars` and `./validators` are typed (`types/auth/*.d.ts`, `types/plugins.d.ts`, `types/scalars.d.ts`, `types/validators.d.ts`, each deriving its members from `types/index.d.ts` via `typeof`; `typesVersions` for node10); `./internal/*` stays undeclared. The root also exports `UnauthenticatedError` and `ForbiddenError`, the same classes as the `auth` ones, declared with an explicit `export class`; `types/auth/errors.d.ts` re-exports those root classes.
- `packages/mongodb` publishes as `@simtlix/simfinity-js`, preserving the public name, entry points and existing `src/` deep imports. Its `src/index.js` binds core to the MongoDB adapter. Model generation, pipelines and transactions live under `packages/mongodb/src/mongo/`; helper shims preserve core identities. The `src/` shims of public API have sibling `.d.ts` files: the core-subpath shims' siblings (`src/auth/{index,errors,expressions,rules}`, `src/{plugins,scalars,validators}`) are the shim text without the `register-mongo-object-id` import; `src/index`, `src/mcp`, `src/const/*` and `src/errors/*` are root-derived one-liners. `src/mongo/*` and `register-mongo-object-id.js` stay undeclared.
- Mongo adapters support fixed `referentialIntegrity: 'off' | 'transactional'`, default off. Strict adapters bind to one runtime, require awaited `adapter.initialize()` after schema creation/connection, and audit existing declared references. `mongo/integrity.js` uses core model descriptions, reserved target-document ObjectId lock writes, snapshot/majority transactions and incoming native queries. Save/update checks read persisted post-images; deletion locks the target before checking incoming references. Supplied transactions abort on integrity violations or ambiguous update results. Raw model/driver calls and middleware escaping the session bypass the guarantee. See `docs/guide/mongodb-integrity.md`.
- `packages/mcp` (`@simtlix/simfinity-mcp`) owns database-independent tool generation and transports. Its SDK peer is optional. MongoDB retains compatibility re-exports; core and PostgreSQL do not depend on MCP.
- `packages/sql` (`@simtlix/simfinity-sql`) owns `createSQL`, the pure relational planner, records and session orchestration. It imports core/GraphQL only. The version-1 plugin contract binds one plugin/configuration per instance, rejects missing guarantees before physical compilation/I/O, and never silently drops FKs or constraints. See `docs/guide/sql-plugins.md`.
- `packages/postgres` (`@simtlix/simfinity-postgres`) exposes `postgresPlugin(options)`, `createPostgres({pool,schema})`, the default configure/connect/createSchema facade and schema generation/initialization. It depends on SQL, core and `pg`, never Mongoose/MongoDB/MCP. Backend configuration is bound once and PostgreSQL types must be registered before createSchema.
- PostgreSQL 3.3.0 lowering preserves the 3.2.0 physical description/DDL, protected by golden fixtures, except that `planRelationalSchema` no longer indexes `[ID]` list columns (3.5.6); initialization keeps such legacy indexes and warns once per call with exact-name `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY` statements. Plugin capabilities cover transactions, FKs/deferral, embedded/owned records, scalar lists and NULL-equal uniqueness. Only PostgreSQL is a supported SQL plugin.
- PostgreSQL executes the shared GraphQL lifecycle: scopes, middleware, validators, hooks, nested CRUD and state transitions. Native model/session APIs differ from Mongoose. Supported parity is tested against real databases; do not claim arbitrary Mongo pipeline/model compatibility.
- GraphQL metadata generates real FKs, including inverse collections, link entities and references in privately owned embedded tables. JSONB is used only for value-only embedded subtrees. See `docs/postgresql.md` for rejected shapes and current constraints.
- Generated PostgreSQL functions/triggers are explicit serializable metadata. Initializers verify their exact identities, bodies/configuration and enabled/deferrable enforcement; create mode backfills keys and validates existing data transactionally, never replaces drift. Private guard rows serialize owned mutations; typed key tables enforce owner-aware multikey uniqueness. Deferred triggers validate owned shape, and immutable validators enforce value-only JSONB shapes.
- Embedded queries use JSONB projections for ragged lists and catalog-validated immutable ordering helpers: MIN/MAX compares whole arrays; sorting selects immediate extrema. Owned scalar/reference/JSONB and root JSONB presenceColumn markers distinguish SQL NULL absence from explicit null. Normalize descendant list defaults/minimized inline objects before encoding and deriving unique keys; preserve required constraints. Date parameters use per-query UTC encoding, never global pg defaults.
- Initialization borrows one pool client, owns its transaction, awaits DDL/catalog validation and never destructively synchronizes existing storage. `validate` mode is read-only. PostgreSQL >=15 is required.
- Await initialization before serving. Requests rejected before the plugin keep readiness: `DATABASE_CREATION_DISABLED` (409) is checked on the requested mode (any truthy mode except `validate` on a validation-only runtime, where a falsy mode validates), and `INVALID_INITIALIZATION_MODE` (400) on the effective mode (anything but `create`, `validate` or undefined, so it fires only when creation is allowed); a ready runtime keeps serving while it re-initializes; the most recently started initialization decides readiness and any failure of it marks the instance unavailable. `preventCreatingCollection(true)` before createSchema forces validation only. Text columns and typed query expressions use binary C collation.
- Enum query filters resolve names before strict internal values, then encode the selected value for storage; query normalization must never be applied to writes or state guards. LIKE is string-only.
- PostgreSQL writes use repeatable-read transactions; supplied sessions are joined, owned sessions retry confirmed serialization/deadlock aborts after a full-jitter backoff that starts once the client is released. Shared typed input snapshots isolate attempts. Adapter stateValue supplies the canonical persisted representation for state writes/guards (names on Mongo, enum internal values on PostgreSQL).
- Standalone saveObject owns a full workflow transaction including hooks and independent nested entities; active supplied sessions remain caller-owned. Owner+embedded reads use one snapshot. Only ownership FKs cascade.

## Core Flow

```
Define GraphQLObjectType
       |
       v
simfinity.connect(null, Type, 'singular', 'plural', controller, onModelCreated, stateMachine)
  -- or --
simfinity.addNoEndpointType(Type)   // supporting types without endpoints
       |
       v
simfinity.createSchema()
       |
       +-- assertCollectionConnections() // INVALID_MODEL for collections without connectionField
       +-- storedIdentity flags       // entities: connect() types and relation targets
       +-- adapter.prepare()          // prepare storage metadata
       +-- adapter.createModel()      // Mongoose models or PostgreSQL handles
       +-- installIdResolver()        // entity `id` -> `_id ?? id`
       +-- autoGenerateResolvers()    // wire relationship resolvers
       +-- installEmbeddedValueResolvers() // only when adapter.readEmbeddedValue exists; readEmbeddedField also nulls data-less objects that miss a required member (readEmbeddedObject, which reads stored members through adapter.rawEmbeddedValue, never getters; MongoDB returns hydrated values whose schema declares getters, virtuals, aliases or subdocument methods inside them unchanged, so they are kept)
       +-- buildRootQuery()           // list, single-entity, aggregate queries
       +-- buildMutation()            // resolve pending inputs and build mutations
       |
       v
  GraphQLSchema
       |
       +-- PostgreSQL: await initializeDatabase({ mode: 'create' | 'validate' })
       |
       v
  Serve with Yoga/Apollo after database startup
```

