### MCP contracts

- Generated input schemas preserve nullability independently from requiredness, including list items and recursive `$defs`. Defaults use GraphQL AST serialization; enum literals use member names. Opaque defaults without a literal representation use the original field default when omitted. The top-level arguments object is closed (`additionalProperties: false`); `$defs` are not.
- The terminal rejects with `MCP_UNKNOWN_ARGUMENT` (an isError result, before default-page injection, `graphql()` or `fetch`) only the undeclared keys the caller sent: the `callTool` wrapper records them, with defined values, in a `WeakMap` keyed by the `call` object before middleware runs, so `composeToolMiddleware` must hand the terminal that same object. Keys middleware injects pass; caller keys middleware deletes pass (the terminal checks `Object.hasOwn`, so a deleted `constructor` or `__proto__` is not mistaken for the inherited member). `suggestArgument` hints a case-insensitive match, else distance <= 1 when either name has <= 4 characters and <= 2 otherwise, never `id` except case-insensitively.
- `maxResultBytes` caps the serialized logical payload on success and on errors, including partial data and `totalCount`. Limit diagnostics (`MCP_RESULT_TOO_LARGE`, `MCP_PAGE_SIZE_EXCEEDED`, `MCP_UNKNOWN_ARGUMENT`) are exempt; envelope overhead, duplicate structured content, remote download size and middleware-created results are outside the cap. An oversized mutation result (`entry.kind === 'mutation'`) never reads as a failure that invites a retry: `oversizedMutationResult` returns `isError: false`, `_meta.truncated` and `{ [field]: {id} | [{id}], [truncationKey]: { applied, message, resultBytes, maxResultBytes } }` on success (the ids only when they fit within `Math.floor(maxResultBytes)`, the integer the notice reports because `outputSchema` declares one; caps themselves may be fractional), and keeps `MCP_RESULT_TOO_LARGE` with `extensions.applied` (`true` or `'unknown'`, never `false`), `firstError` (300 characters) and id `data` with GraphQL errors. Its wording follows `entry.op` and `entry.idempotent` (the merged `idempotentHint`). Queries keep the plain error, plus `extensions.totalCount` on counted lists.
- Result keys MCP adds next to the root field use `resultKey` (`__`-prefixed on collision): `totalCount` for counted calls of list tools with a `pagination` argument (declared in `outputSchema`; a remote `extensions.count` only when it is a non-negative safe integer), and `truncated` (declared only when `maxResultBytes` is set at setup). Both are declared only without a `selection` override; `outputSchema` never gets `additionalProperties: false`. `_meta.count` stays.
- Generated find fields carry `extensions.simfinityQuery.countSink: true`; core's `canReportCount(parent, info, context)` runs before the queries, and without a sink and with a context that is not an object or function the count query is skipped. `reportCount(parent, info, context, count)` hands the count to a function under `Symbol.for('simfinity.countSink')` on `parent` (the root value), then `info.rootValue`, else stores it with `Reflect.set(context, 'count', count)`, and returns whether it was stored; a refused write logs one `warnOnce(runtimeIdentity, 'count-context', …)`. MCP passes a null-prototype root value with that sink on every in-process call of a flagged list tool and the caller's context unchanged, reading the count only when `pagination.count === true`. Unflagged list tools keep the `Object.create(context)` layer on counted calls. `buildToolDefinitions` reads the flag from the field at setup, before `schemaPlugins`, not from the resolver that runs: a replaced or wrapped resolver on a flagged field must forward `parent` or `info`, or report through the sink, unless the application clears the flag before setup. Neither package exports the symbol.
- Check cancellation again after awaiting an in-process context factory, before calling GraphQL. Do not promise to undo database work already started.
- Remote attempt timeouts include reading the body. Queries alone may retry transport failures; client cancellation rejects during fetch, body reading or a retry backoff (`sleep(ms, signal)` rejects with `signal.reason` and removes its listener when the timer fires). Validate the remote GraphQL envelope before creating a tool result. `combineAbortSignals` returns `{ signal, release }`: it uses `AbortSignal.any` when the runtime has it, and on Node.js 19 and 20.0–20.2, inside the engines range, abort listeners that remove themselves on abort and through `release()`, which the retry loop calls in `finally`, so a long-lived caller signal keeps none. Over `createHTTPMCPHandler`, a client's `notifications/cancelled` reaches a fresh per-request server that does not know the request id, so only `res.on('close')` (`server.close()`) aborts an in-flight call's `extra.signal`; do not document MCP cancellation as reaching calls there.
- Remote `endpoint`, `headers` and `timeoutMs` are read on every call. `buildRemoteHeaders` accepts plain objects (`Object.entries`), Headers, Maps and pairs, sets Content-Type once case-insensitively, returns lowercase names, and reports invalid entries without values (`MCP_REMOTE_REQUEST_FAILED`). `normalizeTimeoutMs` treats falsy and 0 as no timeout, rounds up and caps at 2147483647. `resolveExecution` throws `MCP_INVALID_EXECUTION_CONFIG` at setup only for an `endpoint` that `parseEndpoint` reads as an absolute URL with a username or password (fetch rejects it on every call; the message never quotes the URL): relative, unparsable and non-http(s) endpoints, and `Request` instances, pass setup because an application-provided fetch may serve them. It also throws for invalid `timeoutMs` and for headers containers that are truthy non-objects, promises, functions or one-shot iterators, reading no header value. `readForSetupCheck` skips `headers` and `timeoutMs` when they are own getters (`hasOwnGetter`) or when reading them throws (an inherited getter or a Proxy whose token is not loaded yet), leaving the check to `executeRemote` per call; a non-throwing inherited getter is read and checked at setup. `executeRemote` reports a fetch `TypeError` for an unusable endpoint through `remoteEndpointProblem` (a `Request` uses its own URL), never with the undici message that quotes the URL.
- `createHTTPMCPHandler` always creates a fresh stateless server/transport per POST request; any other method gets 405 with `Allow: POST` and a JSON-RPC -32000 body inside the handler's `try`, before `context`, `callTool`, the server or the transport exist. Reject session generators, session callbacks and event stores at setup, after the SDK and transport load and before `resolveExecution`; applications needing sessions manage a transport with `createMCPServer`.
- `composeToolMiddleware` turns a synchronous middleware throw into a rejection and marks every dispatched promise handled (`pending.catch(() => {})`) while returning it.
- `classifyOperation`: queries use `extensions.simfinityQuery`, then the `aggregation` argument; a list needs `pagination` and a single record `id`, otherwise `customQuery` (read-only). Mutations use core's `extensions.simfinityMutation` (`save`/`update`/`delete`/`state_changed` → `transition`/`custom_mutation`), else the placeholder description plus name prefix; never the name alone. `buildAnnotations` makes updates `idempotentHint: !acceptsAddedItems(field)` (a list input field named `added` at any depth) and keeps deletes destructive and idempotent (data-level; a repeat calls `onDelete(null)`).
- `prepareMCPTools()` and `createHTTPMCPHandler` validate execution, `toolMiddleware` (`normalizeToolMiddleware`: a single function is a one-element stack, copied at setup; an index loop rejects non-functions and sparse-array holes with `MCP_INVALID_MIDDLEWARE`), `limits` (`normalizeLimits`: falsy means unset, caps go through `normalizeCap` and must be at least 1, one NaN warning per call; then `validateDefaultPagination` runs `coerceInputValue` against each distinct `paginationType`; `MCP_INVALID_LIMITS`) and tool definitions before `applySchemaPlugins()`. That validates every entry (`normalizeSchemaPlugins`, `MCP_INVALID_SCHEMA_PLUGIN`) before any hook runs, inspecting own and inherited property descriptors (`pluginDescriptors`) so no getter other than `onSchemaChange` runs (`then` is checked through its descriptor; a `then` method or getter is rejected as a promise), rejects a misspelling of `onSchemaChange` only on entries without a function `onSchemaChange` (`isMisspelledSchemaHook`: a method or getter key that starts with `onschema` ignoring case or is within two optimal-string-alignment edits of `onschemachange`), warns once per plugin object about ignored hooks, rejects a different schema in `replaceSchema` (`MCP_UNSUPPORTED_SCHEMA_REPLACEMENT`) and returns a promise for asynchronous hooks: the server and HTTP factories await it, and `generateMCPTools` calls wait for it. `callTool` keeps the caller's `limits` object and runs `readLimits` on every call (the HTTP handler re-reads `options.limits` per request), so later changes apply. `createMCPServer`, `startStdioMCPServer` (also `loadStdioTransport`) and `createHTTPMCPHandler` (also its transport export, then its `transportOptions` check) load the SDK before any of this, so SDK errors precede every option error and plugins.
- `scalarToJSONSchema` follows the whole `baseScalarType` chain (cycle-safe) for the input JSON type and `format`, and publishes a custom scalar's own description. With `{ output: true }` it publishes a primitive type only for spec scalars reached through `createValidatedScalar` links (`serializedSpecScalar`, which follows core's non-exported `Symbol.for('simfinity.validatedScalarBase')` marker); every other scalar is `{}`, with a curated description for description-less `Date`/`DateTime`/`Time` roots. `includeId` is accepted and ignored.
- TypeScript (`packages/mcp/types/index.d.ts`): keep the read-side option interfaces (`GenerateMCPToolsOptions`, `MCPServerOptions`, `HTTPMCPHandlerOptions`, `MCPLimits`, `MCPRemoteExecutionOptions`) at their 3.5.6 shapes, and widen accepted input only in the input-only parameter types (`MCPOptionsInit`, `MCPLimitsInit`, `MCPRemoteExecutionInit`, `GenerateMCPToolsInit`, `MCPServerInit`, `HTTPMCPHandlerInit`), so code that reads options back keeps compiling. The `mcpTypes` consumer in `scripts/test-packages.js` pins both directions.

## Instance State (inside `createRuntime` in `packages/core/src/runtime.js`)


- `typesDict` -- primary registry: `{ types: { [typeName]: { model, gqltype, simpleEntityEndpointName, listEntitiesEndpointName, endpoint, controller, stateMachine, inputType, storedIdentity } } }`. `storedIdentity` is set on every `createSchema()` before `adapter.prepare()`: true for `connect()` types and targets of non-embedded relations (entities), false for embedded-only types; it is mirrored on `typesDictForUpdate`, and entity type objects are also marked with `markStoredIdentity()`, which the Mongo query builder reads (`hasStoredIdentity()`) to map embedded `.id` paths, so the rule follows the shared type object
- `typesDictForUpdate` -- parallel registry with update-specific input types
- `waitingInputType` -- registered types whose inputs are not built yet; a type waits only for the input types of its embedded fields. Types with unsupported writable lists (lists of lists) or embedded types that contain each other never resolve (`INPUT_TYPE_UNRESOLVED`, naming every unbuildable field of the listed types, with the causes apart from the fields that only wait for another listed type). Embedded cycles reach this with adapters that do not validate models, and in default MongoDB mode when only supplied models reach the cycle (a `readOnly` link then builds). PostgreSQL and transactional MongoDB (`describeModels`, every entity) and generated MongoDB models (`generateSchemaDefinition`, every mode, only the models it generates) reject them first with `INVALID_MODEL` `Embedded cycle at …`; do not add a prepare-time walk in 3.x, since supplied-model cycles with a `readOnly` link start today. Referenced collections resolve item inputs lazily, in fields thunks, so they never wait
- `excludedFieldInputTypes` -- `Map` from generated `added` item input name to `{ itemType, fieldToExclude, optionalFields, type }`, so collections that reach one child through one `connectionField` share that input
- `selfCollectionNameHolders` and `qualifiedSelfCollections` -- `Map`s from a self-referencing collection field name to the types whose inputs were built with `OneToManyA|U<field>`, and from a type to its self collections named `OneToMany<Type>A|U<field>` (`claimSelfCollectionNames()`). A holder qualifies another type only while it is still the registered type for its name and the same build reaches it; otherwise the type being built becomes another holder
- `registeredMutations` -- custom mutations registered via `registerMutation()`
- `middlewares` -- array of middleware functions registered on this runtime via `use()`; `use()` rejects non-functions with `INVALID_MIDDLEWARE`
- `runtimeIdentity` -- private object identifying this runtime in `runtimeBoundTypes`
- `reportedIssues` -- `WeakMap` from type to the `Configuration issue` warning keys already logged (`warnOnce`), so each warning is logged once per runtime

These registries are per runtime. Module-scope state shared by every runtime:

- the introspection extension;
- `maxPageSize` in `packages/core/src/query-limits.js` (`configureQueryLimits()`);
- `maxNestedOperations` in `packages/core/src/mutation-limits.js` (`configureMutationLimits()`), null (unlimited) by default;
- `runtimeBoundTypes` in `runtime.js`, a `WeakMap` from each GraphQL object type to the identity of the runtime that generated its relation resolvers (bound) or whose schema reached it with an unresolved non-embedded relation field (reserved, such as an unregistered custom mutation result). `connect()`, `addNoEndpointType()` and `createSchema()` throw `TYPE_BOUND_TO_OTHER_RUNTIME` (409) for a type bound or reserved elsewhere;
- `relationFieldOwners` in `packages/core/src/relation-owners.js` (internal, imported by `runtime.js` and `auth/index.js`), a `WeakMap` from each generated relation resolver, the fresh extensions object of its field, and the auth plugin's wrapper of such a resolver (which inherits the owner) to the runtime that generated it. `toConfig()` copies and field spreads keep the resolver and the extensions object, so `createSchema()` and `autoGenerateResolvers()` reject a copied field from another runtime (`assertFieldsNotBoundElsewhere`): fields without a resolver are skipped and get this runtime's own; a foreign resolver tag is rejected; the extensions tag counts only on fields whose resolver is untagged; interface fields are not checked, because graphql-js never runs their resolvers (the types they reference still are). A third-party in-place wrapper copied with a rebuilt extensions object is not detected. Type objects are not shared between runtimes that generate or reserve their relation resolvers; types without unresolved non-embedded relation fields may be, and a `toConfig()` copy made before the first schema may be used by another runtime.

Do not describe module-scope state as instance-isolated.

## Introspection Extension (Critical)

Simfinity adds `extensions` to the field map returned by `__Field.getFields()`. This works before or after GraphQL materializes introspection fields and reuses an existing extension field on repeated module evaluation, preserving metadata type identity. Do not capture or replace the private `_fields` thunk.

The extension is still global to the shared GraphQL peer, and Simfinity's registries belong to each runtime instance. Schemas constructed after import collect `FieldExtensionsType` and `RelationType` (or their fallback names `SimfinityFieldExtensionsType` and `SimfinityRelationType`, which a clash in the process's first Simfinity schema sets for every later schema) in their type maps. Pre-existing schemas retain their original type maps: ordinary operations and direct metadata introspection work, but named fragments on these new types require a schema constructed after import.

Tools that clone types (`graphql-middleware`'s `applyMiddleware` / `mapSchema`) remain unsupported. `buildClientSchema()` on a post-import schema's introspection can also produce duplicate metadata types when run in the same process as Simfinity. Use Envelop plugins and in-place resolver wrapping; initialization safety does not provide schema isolation or schema-cloning compatibility.
